## Why This Is Asked
This question tests proficiency with partition dynamic programming where subarrays must be non-overlapping and values derive from range statistics. The solution requires careful separation of concerns: precomputing range queries, implementing the DP transition, and reconstructing the solution through pointer tracking.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping contiguous subarrays as partition elements
- Dynamic programming with two-dimensional state tracking
- Range minima preprocessing for O(1) query access
- Solution reconstruction through backpointers
- Complexity optimization through careful algorithm design

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysMin(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;

  // Precompute range minima
  const mins = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let m = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      m = Math.min(m, A[j]);
      mins[i][j] = m;
    }
  }
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with subarray composition where each block incurs a fixed cost, a realistic pattern in scheduling/payments. It also checks reconstruction of intervals.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums to compute subarray sums fast
- DP over positions and block counts
- Optimization: collapse inner max with a running best value per t
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraySumMinusC(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const endAt = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = Array(k+1).fill(-1e15);
  const bestIdx = Array(k+1).fill(-1);
  bestVal[1] = 0; bestIdx[1] = 1; // allow start at 1 for first block
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
      const endVal = pref[i] - c + bestVal[t];
      if (dp[i-1][t] >= endVal) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = endVal;
        endAt[i][t] = {start: bestIdx[t], end: i};
      }
    }
    for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
      const cand = dp[i][t-1] - pref[i];
      if (cand > bestVal[t]) { bestVal[t] = cand; bestIdx[t] = i+1; }
    }
  }
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const e = endAt[i][t];
    if (e) { blocks.push([e.start, e.end]); i = e.start - 1; t--; }
    else { i--; }
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at most k blocks or variable per-block cost?
- How does the reconstruction change if ties occur on values?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to model selections of multiple disjoint subarrays with an exact count — a common dynamic programming pattern in interviews.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefix**: State dp[i][t] tracks the best total using the first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) calculation of any subarray sum
- **Backpointers**: Store decisions to reconstruct the chosen subarrays
- **Edge cases**: Handle when n < k or when all values are negative

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skip: true };
      }
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (t > 0 && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t - 1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const p = prev[i][t];
    if (p.skip) { i--; continue; }
    result.push([p.l, i]);
    i = p.l - 1; t = p.t;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model multi-block dynamic programming with non-trivial block scoring that depends on range statistics (max, min) and block length. It also covers reconstruction of the exact blocks, and invites discussion of time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Partition DP with fixed k blocks
- Block score uses range (max-min) and length
- Inline maintenance of max/min while expanding the last block
- Path reconstruction with predecessor pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScore(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let curMax = -INF, curMin = INF;
      for (let l = i; l >= 1; l--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[l-1]);
        curMin = Math.min(curMin, A[l-1]);
        const blockScore = (curMax - curMin) * (i - l + 1);
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore;
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = parent[i][t] + 1;
    blocks.unshift([l, i]);
    i = l - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return {maxScore: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity

Time: O(n²k) - for each i and t, we scan O(n) possible l values while maintaining max/min.

Space: O(nk) for dp table plus O(nk) for parent pointers if reconstruction needed, otherwise O(nk).

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of dynamic programming on prefixes with a non-crossing pairing constraint. The core challenge is encoding the no-crossing condition by considering the last pair ending at position i with its left partner p.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefixes**: Extra dimension tracks the number of pairs formed
- **Non-crossing constraint**: Enforced by pairing i with p < i and using dp[p-1][t-1], which ensures all pairs lie completely to the left of p
- **Reconstruction**: Requires storing backpointers to output the actual pairs
- **Complexity**: Time O(n²k), Space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPairs(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      prev[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let p = 1; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p - 1][t - 1] + A[p - 1] * A[i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skip: false, p };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t].skip) {
      i--;
    } else {
      const p = prev[i][t].p;
      pairs.push([p, i]);
      i = p - 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs: pairs.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a grid-path DP with a bounded number of teleports, forcing state compression and a 2D prefix max to keep complexity at O(KRC). It tests correctness, backtracking, and memory considerations beyond standard path DP.

## Key Concepts
- DP on grid with at most k teleports
- 2D prefix max to propagate bestPrev for teleport transitions
- Reconstruction: store choice or re-run DP with pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxEnergy(grid, k) {
  const R = grid.length, C = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  let dpPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
  dpPrev[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const bestPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        bestPrev[r][c] = dpPrev[r][c];
        if (r > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r][c-1]);
      }
    }
    const dpCurr = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        if (r === 0 && c === 0) continue;
        let best = NEG;
        if (r > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r][c-1]);
        if (bestPrev[r][c] !== NEG) best = Math.max(best, bestPrev[r][c]);
        if (best !== NEG) dpCurr[r][c] = best + grid[r][c];
      }
    }
    dpPrev = dpCurr;
  }
  return dpPrev[R-1][C-1];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What is the time/space complexity and how would you optimize for very large grids?
- How would you implement robust path reconstruction for the teleport usages?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a DP with two choices per step (1- or 2-length blocks), plus reconstruction and complexity awareness.

## Key Concepts

- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Transitions for 1-length and 2-length blocks
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      // 1-length block ending at i-1
      if(dp[i-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 1; }
      }
      // 2-length block ending at i-1
      if(i>=2 && dp[i-2][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-2][t-1] + A[i-2] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 2; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back from (n, k)
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to reconstruct the exact block endpoints efficiently?
- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a non-trivial DP over a linear sequence with a non-crossing constraint and reconstruction of the chosen pairs. It balances readability with the need to optimize across many left endpoints efficiently.

## Key Concepts

- Non-crossing pairings on a line
- DP by right endpoint with reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs; potential 1D optimization for i dimension

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonCrossingPairs(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      let best = i>0 ? dp[i-1][t] : 0;
      let bestJ = -1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const left = (j>=1) ? dp[j-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = left + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(val > best){ best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best;
      take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction sketch (omitted for brevity)
  return { value: dp[n-1][k], pairs: [] };
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce exactly k pairs instead of at most k?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n) or derive a monotone queue optimization for certain A distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend Kadane-like DP to k non-overlapping segments with per-element penalties and reconstruction of exact endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP for k disjoint subarrays with penalties
- Local/global state separation for endings vs global maxima
- Backpointer reconstruction to recover endpoints
- Time complexity O(nk), space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, p) {
  const n = A.length;
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  const global = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  global[0][0] = 0;
  // backpointers omitted for brevity in this snippet
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = A[i - 1] - p;
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i - 1][t] + val, global[i - 1][t - 1] + val);
      global[i][t] = Math.max(global[i - 1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return { maxValue: global[n][k], endpoints: [] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow at most k subarrays or to output the exact endpoints with a deterministic tie-breaker?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a novel fixed-gap non-crossing pairing constraint, a natural extension of classic non-crossing matching problems, while probing DP state design and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with fixed pair distance
- Non-crossing/disjoint constraint handling
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Space/time optimization trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxFixedGapPairs(A, d, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i - d - 1 >= 0) {
        const cand = dp[i - d - 1][t - 1] + A[i - 1] * A[i - d - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cand;
          take[i][t] = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      pairs.push([i - d, i]); // 1-based indices of the pair
      i = i - d - 1;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  pairs.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new DP angle: selecting exactly k non-overlapping substrings with a per-substring palindrome-anagram constraint, requiring fast feasibility testing via prefix masks and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Palindromable substrings via 26-bit parity mask
- Prefix XOR to test s[l..r] in O(1)
- DP over i and t with transitions from p-1
- Backpointer reconstruction; space optimization with rolling arrays
- Time complexity: O(n^2 k)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLenPalAnagram(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] ^ (1 << (s.charCodeAt(i - 1) - 97));
  function popcount(x) { let c = 0; while (x) { x &= x - 1; c++; } return c; }
  function canPal(l, r) {
    return popcount(pref[r] ^ pref[l - 1]) <= 1;
  }
  // DP skeleton: dp[i][t] = max total length through i with t substrings
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to near O(nk) for special input patterns? 
- How to reconstruct actual substrings efficiently and handle ties?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a 3D state on a grid path, a natural but less common extension of standard grid DP.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP on a grid, path constraints (only right/down), exact boosts
- Bottom-up iteration, reconstruction pointers, space/time considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBoostOnGrid(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        const fromTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t] : NEG;
        const fromLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t] : NEG;
        let best = Math.max(fromTop, fromLeft);
        if (best > NEG/2) dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
        if (t > 0) {
          const hotTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1] : NEG;
          const hotLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1] : NEG;
          const bestPrev = Math.max(hotTop, hotLeft);
          if (bestPrev > NEG/2) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], bestPrev + 2*grid[i][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][k];
}
